  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1222 PM MDT Sat Jul 25 2026\n\nOur pattern remains locked in for the next few days. Monsoon\nmoisture will keep showers and thunderstorms around. The best\nchance for development today is fairly similar to yesterday,\nacross the South Hills/Albion Mountains, southeast highlands,\nthrough Driggs and the Teton Valley. A few storms are also\nexpected from Mackay through Gilmore Summit and into southwest\nMontana. There is a MARGINAL RISK for severe thunderstorms again\nfor most of eastern Idaho due to strong outflow winds. There is\na 10-40% chance of gusts over 35 mph across the central\nmountains, and up to 90% elsewhere, with highest gusts over 60 \nmph. Outside of storms, gusts today are forecast in the 25-35\nmph range. Overnight, we expect the focus for light showers to\nshift to the central mountains and Lava Beds around Shoshone. We\ncurrently are not putting thunderstorms in the forecast, the\nchance is NOT ZERO. For tomorrow, a few showers and storms are\nexpected across the central mountains and along the Montana\nborder. Another area should be from around Malta to Driggs.\nThere is a 20-60% chance for gusts over 35 mph. Wind gusts\noutside of storms will be similar to what we are seeing today. A\nHEAT ADVISORY remains in effect for the Snake Plain, Magic\nValley, and South Hills/Albion Mountains. We have extended it\nthrough tomorrow evening, as well as added valley locations down\ntoward the Utah border through Malad and the Cache Valley. Highs\nwill be mainly in the 90s to around 100 degrees. \n\nThe pattern remains fairly unchanged through Tuesday. The main \nchance for showers and storms will remain outside of the central\nmountains.Temperatures do back off slightly, which may put us \njust under any thresholds for continuing headlines for HeatRisk.\nAlthough all it will take is for temperatures to creep back up \na few degrees and we will be back into Heat Advisory territory.\nThe models are again trending drier, maybe not 100% storm-free\ninitially, but still drier for the end of the upcoming week and\nnext weekend. The ridge is forecast to shift north and west,\npush the bulk of the monsoon moisture well to our south. This\nmay initially drop temperatures back a bit more, but return us\nto similar high temperatures to what we are seeing right now.\nThe pattern does look breezy, which along with hot and dry\nconditions may influence fire weather concerns across the area.",
